WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.750
Mitigation of user-prompt bias in large language models.

2
00:00:03.800 --> 00:00:07.050
A user's prompt can carry a stereotype. A language model that accepts the premise can repeat it back with authority.

3
00:00:07.100 --> 00:00:11.450
The framework puts a bias detector in front of the model, so a stereotyped prompt is flagged before it is answered.

4
00:00:11.500 --> 00:00:16.150
The detector learns from CrowS-Pairs: 1,508 pairs of sentences that differ only in the group they mention.

5
00:00:16.200 --> 00:00:20.650
The pairs cover nine kinds of bias, from race and gender to age, disability and physical appearance.

6
00:00:20.700 --> 00:00:23.750
Step one: RoBERTa's byte-pair tokenizer splits the prompt into subword pieces. 'Teenagers' becomes 'teen' plus 'agers'.

7
00:00:23.800 --> 00:00:26.150
Step two: the RoBERTa encoder turns every token into a vector that depends on its context.

8
00:00:26.200 --> 00:00:28.950
Step three: a recurrent network reads the vectors in order, carrying a memory of what it has read so far.

9
00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:32.150
Step four: the last state becomes a probability that the prompt is stereotyped.

10
00:00:32.200 --> 00:00:37.050
Three recurrent readers are compared. A plain RNN overwrites its memory at every word; an LSTM and a GRU use gates to decide what to keep.

11
00:00:37.100 --> 00:00:41.350
Each model is trained on 80% of the data, tuned on 10%, and tested once on the remaining 10%.

12
00:00:41.400 --> 00:00:46.050
On the held-out test split, RoBERTa with a GRU is the most accurate: 93%, with precision 0.92 and F1 0.90.

13
00:00:46.100 --> 00:00:49.050
The plain RNN has the highest recall, 0.89, but the lowest precision.

14
00:00:49.100 --> 00:00:54.350
The GRU's precision comes from few false alarms: it flags only 7% of non-stereotyped examples, against 10% and 12%.

15
00:00:54.400 --> 00:00:59.250
Flag the stereotype before the model answers. Next: data beyond U.S. stereotypes, and real user prompts.
